<section class="section" id="quickstart">
  <h2>Quickstart</h2>
  <p>Install the CLI, hand it your key, and point it at a change plus the test that's supposed to guard it:</p>
  <pre class="code">go install github.com/pdbethke/corralai/cmd/corral@latest
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
corral certify --local --code path/to/file.go \
  --goal "what it must guarantee" -- go test ./...</pre>
  <p>
    Out comes a signed verdict — <strong>certified</strong> or
    <strong>needs-review</strong> — with the kill-rate, the survivors, and any
    written-and-proven killing test. Re-check the record offline, anytime:
  </p>
  <pre class="code">corral certify verify record.json    # re-runs the proof — no key, no network</pre>
  <p>
    The herd runs in a bwrap jail on Linux (<code>--jail container</code> for
    docker/podman elsewhere) and grades tests in five languages — Go, Python,
    Ruby, JavaScript, TypeScript — writing its killing test in
    <em>your</em> project's harness, so vitest, jest, pytest and minitest work
    without configuration. Roles are decorrelated across models by default, and
    each role can be pointed at a different <em>vendor</em>: a Gemini
    fault-planter with a Claude critic is a supported run, off your own keys.
  </p>
  <p class="caveat">
    Honest about the coverage behind that: <strong>Go and Python are exercised
    hardest</strong> — Go continuously in this repo's own CI, Python across
    repeated whole-repo scans of Flask. TypeScript, JavaScript and Ruby each
    rest on a <strong>single third-party repository</strong> so far, which shows
    the plugin works and is not evidence about the ecosystem.
    <a href="https://github.com/pdbethke/corralai#readme">The evidence table is in the README →</a>
  </p>
</section>

